        <section id="coverage">
            <h2>3. Engineering for Coverage: Particle Size and Distribution</h2>
            <p class="intro-para">
                The efficacy of a disinfectant is fundamentally physical before it is chemical. The engineering challenge within the aerosol can is to optimize the "Mean Droplet Diameter" (particle size) to ensure every crevice of a textured surface is penetrated by the antimicrobial solution.
            </p>
            <p>
                For surface-acting aerosols, the target particle size is typically between 40 and 80 microns. Droplets smaller than this (sub-10 microns) may drift as "dry fog," failing to wet the surface adequately. Droplets larger than 150 microns create "coalescence" or puddling, which leads to wasteful runoff and potential damage to sensitive electronic equipment in healthcare settings.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Surface Tension Reduction:</strong> Professional formulations incorporate non-ionic surfactants to allow the liquid to "crawl" into microscopic pits and textures.</li>
                <li><strong>Pressure and Valve Dynamics:</strong> The internal pressure, combined with the "Vapor Tap" on the valve, dictates the degree of mechanical breakup during the split-second transition from liquid to spray.</li>
                <li><strong>Actuator Selection:</strong> The final spray pattern—whether a fine mist or a wide cone—is determined by the internal geometry of the actuator's swirl chamber.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="regulatory">
            <h2>4. Navigating the Regulatory Landscape (Global &amp; India)</h2>
            <p class="intro-para">
                A disinfectant aerosol is not a cosmetic; in most jurisdictions, it is classified either as a "Drug" (India) or a "Biocide/Pesticide" (USA/EU). This classification demands rigorous third-party laboratory verification of all antimicrobial claims before the product can be commercially released.
            </p>
            <p>
                In the United States, the <strong>Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)</strong> manages disinfectant registrations under FIFRA. In India, the <strong>CDSCO (Central Drugs Standard Control Organization)</strong> governs these products under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, specifically under "Schedule O" for disinfectants.
            </p>

            <h3>4.1 Indian Compliance: CDSCO and Schedule 'O'</h3>
            <p>Manufacturers must demonstrate compliance with Schedule 'O' standards, which specify testing against specific bacterial strains. The technical dossier must include stability tests (proving the active remains potent at the end of shelf life) and AOAC efficacy data.</p>

            <h3>4.2 EPA List N and Global Standards</h3>
            <p>Products intended for healthcare use in the US must be registered on specialized lists like "List N." This requires testing against harder-to-kill pathogens (surrogates) according to the <strong>AOAC 961.02 Germicidal Spray Test</strong> protocols which prove a 100% kill across specified replicates.</p>

